# Never let a real safe, a key, or a decrypted anything into the repo.
*.kdbx
*.kdb
*.psafe3
*.psafe3.bak
*.key
*.keyx
*.pem
*.p12
*.lock
*.plk

# The throwaway fixture safes ARE committed: every passphrase in
# tests/fixtures/manifest.json is published on purpose and none of these files
# holds a real credential. Without these negations the blanket bans above eat
# them and the suite ships with nothing to test against.
!tests/fixtures/**/*.kdbx
!tests/fixtures/**/*.psafe3
!tests/fixtures/**/*.key
# `.keyx` is a DIFFERENT extension from `.key` and the pattern above does not
# match it. Two of the fixtures are key-file safes; without this line their key
# files are ignored, they are committed unopenable, and the failure only shows
# up as an unlock that cannot be made to work.
!tests/fixtures/**/*.keyx

# The malformed corpus is GENERATED, not curated: `tests/corpus/gen_corpus.py
# --build --clean` rebuilds all 67 cases in about 3.5 s from the committed
# fixtures. Ignoring the whole directory is deliberate — committing the
# `.expect.json` sidecars while the data files they describe were silently
# ignored (which is what the blanket bans above did) is worse than committing
# neither, because `--check` then reports a corpus that is not there.
tests/corpus/files/

# Built artefact: tests/oracle/build.sh rebuilds it offline from the module
# cache in seconds. 3.5 MB of Go binary does not belong in a source tree.
tests/oracle/pws3_oracle

secrets.log
audit/
backups/
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.playwright/
test-results/
_test_output.txt
